## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical integration of workload identity with policy enforcement in a CNF gateway, emphasizing data governance, per-tenant isolation, and non-disruptive upgrades. It requires concrete steps for credential rotation, policy encoding, runtime checks, and observability.

## Key Concepts

- SPIRE SVIDs and workload attestation for multitenant isolation
- OPA Gatekeeper policy modeling per tenant (retention windows, redaction rules)
- Runtime log redaction and per-tenant observability
- Kind-based local testbed for end-to-end verification
- Zero-downtime rollout with traffic shaping and drift checks

## Code Example

```javascript
function redactLog(entry, policy) {
  const redacted = { ...entry };
  (policy.redactFields || []).forEach(field => {
    if (redacted.hasOwnProperty(field)) redacted[field] = "***REDACTED***";
  });
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you rotate SVIDs across tenants with revocation semantics in SPIRE?
- How would you validate redaction coverage across logs in CI/CD and production?

## Why This Is Asked
Auditing and non-repudiation in CNF gateways are critical for multi-tenant environments. This question validates practical expertise with Kubernetes Secrets, HMAC signing, sidecar log pipelines, and retention configuration—essential skills for implementing post-deployment security controls.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped auditing and non-repudiation
- HMAC signing using per-tenant Kubernetes Secrets
- Sidecar-based log forwarding to in-cluster sinks
- ConfigMap-driven log retention and namespace scoping

## Code Example
```javascript
const crypto = require('crypto');
function signLog(logLine, secret) {
  return crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret)
    .update(logLine)
    .digest('hex');
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design runtime policy reconfiguration with multi-tenant separation and zero-downtime updates.

## Key Concepts
- Central policy streaming via gRPC
- Per-tenant policy data model with versioning
- In-memory delta application with TTL and idempotence
- CRD design for policy declarations and rollout path

## Code Example
```proto
syntax = "proto3";
package policy;
message PolicyDelta {
  string tenant_id = 1;
  string path_hash = 2;
  uint32 rate_limit = 3;
  uint32 window_seconds = 4;
  uint32 circuit_failure_limit = 5;
  uint32 circuit_reset_seconds = 6;
  uint32 log_sample_rate = 7;
  string policy_version = 8;
}
service PolicyService {
  rpc StreamPolicy(PolicyDelta) returns (stream PolicyDelta);
}
```

